=5"}], "model": {"prior_margin": -6.77, "prior_sd": 5.0, "poll_margin": -6.52, "poll_sd": 4.54, "poll_weight": 0.55, "fundamentals": {"margin": -0.87, "lean": -5.0, "environment": 7.47, "incumbency": -3.0, "money": -0.34}, "rating_margin": -10.7, "sigma_race": 4.8, "sigma_total": 5.54, "baseline_margin": -6.63, "analyst_adjustment": -0.07, "key_factors": ["Polls since late September consistently put Moody ahead by 2 to 11 points, averaging about 6-7", "Florida's R+5 lean and Moody's incumbent statewide profile favor Republicans", "Expert ratings cluster at Likely R and prediction markets price Nixon near 10 percent", "Nixon's third-quarter fundraising edge and Sanders-backed rallies give Democrats a late opening"], "rationale": "Fourteen recent surveys, expert ratings, and prediction markets all point the same way: Moody leads by roughly six to seven points in a state that leans Republican by five. Nixon's strong fundraising and surrogate energy justify keeping this at Likely rather than Solid Republican, but not enough to move the expected margin.", "watch": "Whether Nixon's fundraising advantage and national surrogate attention translate into a late polling shift among lower-propensity voters in the final weeks.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Ashley Moody (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Ashley Moody, appointed to the Senate and running as an incumbent, holds a steady lead over state Representative Angie Nixon in this special election. Polling since late September consistently shows Moody ahead by roughly two to eleven points, averaging about six to seven. Expert raters mostly call the race Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Nixon roughly a one-in-ten chance.\n\nNixon has mounted a strong financial effort, outraising Moody in the third quarter and banking over four million dollars, and Bernie Sanders has headlined rallies on her behalf. Still, Florida's Republican lean and Moody's statewide name recognition frame the contest. Moody rema